    "case_summary": "An 83-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus treated with metformin was admitted for a left foot ulcer secondary to vascular disease. During admission, he was diagnosed with atrial fibrillation and asymptomatic SARS-CoV-2 infection. Seven days after discharge, the patient consulted again, presenting with new skin lesions consisting of violaceous pretibial lesions with areas of necrosis and intense pain in the right lower limb. The patient had been administered subcutaneous abdominal heparin, with no lesion in the site of injection. No history of cocaine use, fever, dyspnea, chest pain, or other systemic symptoms. No previous hospitalizations or heparin administration.\n\nExamination revealed pretibial lesions of violaceous coloration, similar to retiform purpura, below the knee, associated with a necrotic lesion in the right calf. Pedal pulse was present, with poorly defined lesions with evolution of the existing ones to limited partial necrosis.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Platelet count: 173×10⁹/L    * Serum creatinine: 134.37 µmol/L (1.52 mg/dL)    * Glomerular filtration rate: 45.8 mL/min/1.73 m²    * Hemoglobin: 110 g/L    * Leukocytes: 4.75×10⁹/L    * C-reactive protein: 35.8 mg/L    * Autoimmunity studies: negative    * Antiplatelet antibody test: positive for IgG and polivalent antibodies  - Imaging Documentation:    Image Title: Necrotic lesion in the right calf    Image Description: Physical examination showing pretibial lesions of violaceous coloration and necrotic lesion",
